<article class="page-prose">

<div class="page-opening">
<p>Galapagensis is an independent research programme devoted to the history, documentary memory, and knowledge of the Galapagos Islands.</p>

<p>Its starting point is simple: the documentary history of Galapagos does not exist in one place. It is dispersed among archives, libraries, museums, scientific collections, private holdings, publications, photographs, maps, correspondence, material traces, oral memories, and other forms of evidence in Ecuador and around the world.</p>

<p>Galapagensis identifies those fragments, documents them, contextualizes them, connects them, and researches the relationships among them. Its aim is not simply to gather references, but to reconstruct historical contexts and generate new knowledge from a documentary record whose parts are often separated by institutions, countries, formats, disciplines, and time.</p>
</div>

<section id="corpus" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Foundational proposition</p>
<h2>A distributed documentary corpus</h2>

<p>Galapagos documentary heritage is globally dispersed. Galapagensis treats that dispersion not only as a practical difficulty, but as part of the history itself.</p>

<p>A specimen collected in the islands may now be held in a museum thousands of kilometres away. A photograph may survive in a family archive while the expedition it documents appears in institutional correspondence elsewhere. A published account may acquire new meaning when read alongside a ship log, an oral testimony, a map, or an archaeological trace.</p>

<p>Galapagensis therefore does not define its corpus by physical custody. The corpus is created through research: by identifying, describing, contextualizing, and connecting documentary traces that remain in the institutions, collections, communities, and private holdings where they are preserved.</p>

<div class="concept-note">
<p class="eyebrow">Documented relationships</p>
<p>One of the programme’s central intellectual units is the <strong>documented relationship</strong>: a researched connection among sources, people, places, expeditions, objects, specimens, collections, memories, institutions, and later research.</p>
</div>

<p>Galapagensis does not seek to replace the repositories that preserve these materials. It works across them, making relationships visible without suggesting ownership of the underlying originals.</p>
</section>

<section id="history" class="prose-section">
<p class="eyebrow">Genealogy</p>
<h2>History and genealogy</h2>

<p>Galapagensis grows out of a longer programme of documentary and historical research on the Galápagos Islands begun by Edgardo Civallero in 2018, during his work at the Charles Darwin Foundation.</p>

<p>In 2019, that work gave rise to <strong>Galapagueana</strong>, an experimental documentary-history project that explored ways of connecting collections, sources, people, places, historical narratives, and memory. Between 2021 and 2023, part of that work was developed publicly through the Charles Darwin Foundation.</p>

<p>In 2023, Civallero's thesis <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19054301"><em>Los tejedores de memorias</em></a> provided the epistemic foundation that now underlies Galapagensis, developing a broader conception of documentary work as the recovery, connection, interpretation, and activation of heterogeneous fragments of memory and knowledge.</p>

<p>From 2026 onward, Galapagensis develops that framework as an independent research programme whose domain is Galápagos itself, rather than the holdings or perspective of any single institution. Its corpus is constructed across archives, libraries, museums, scientific collections, private holdings, oral testimony, material traces, publications, and communities in Ecuador and internationally.</p>

